 @basiliotornado  The die is basically what the silicon is placed onto. The silicon before it is placed on the die goes through quality control to make sure it meets specifications for the product it is spec'ed for. If it fails to meet specifications but meets or exceeds a lower tiered product's specs, it is binned for that product. This is the standard for both the CPU and GPU industries.

From what I have seen, the dies to the 5090 and 5080 look identical in size. I am not sure about the 5070 though. What can be deduced by that is, if my memory serves me right on their size, both the 5080 and 5090 share the same silicon and either through binning or fusing off sections of it, that is how they get the 5080's GPUs.

AMD's yields with the 9000 series are so high that it is having to fuse off cores to have enough stock for the regular 9070s as opposed to having enough silicon not meeting XT specs.

